WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.265
Deletion from an AVL tree

00:00:02.344 --> 00:00:05.646
is similar to insertion, but more complex.

00:00:05.646 --> 00:00:08.476
First, search downward for the key.

00:00:08.476 --> 00:00:09.105
Second

00:00:09.105 --> 00:00:12.564
delete it as in a plain binary search tree.

00:00:12.564 --> 00:00:14.608
A leaf is simply removed.

00:00:14.686 --> 00:00:16.573
A node with one child is

00:00:16.652 --> 00:00:18.460
replaced by that child.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.812
A node with two children gets

00:00:20.881 --> 00:00:22.607
the key of its successor

00:00:22.607 --> 00:00:25.299
the smallest key in its right subtree

00:00:25.368 --> 00:00:27.646
and then the successor is deleted

00:00:27.715 --> 00:00:29.855
from the right subtree instead.

00:00:29.855 --> 00:00:31.650
It has at most one child.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:32.442
Third

00:00:32.442 --> 00:00:35.540
walk back up the search path to the root

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.678
correcting heights as we go

00:00:37.752 --> 00:00:40.038
and at every node whose balance

00:00:40.112 --> 00:00:42.840
has become +2 or -2, rotate.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:45.336
The difference from insertion

00:00:45.336 --> 00:00:47.911
a deletion makes a subtree shorter

00:00:47.982 --> 00:00:50.557
and after a rotation the subtree may

00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:52.989
still be one shorter than before.

00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:55.778
So the imbalance can propagate upward

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.353
and several rotations may be needed

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.070
on the way to the root.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:04.731
Take the tree 10, 5, 15, with 2

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:08.432
9, 12, 20 below, and 3

00:01:08.432 --> 00:01:11.564
17, 30 at the bottom.

00:01:11.564 --> 00:01:14.411
Every balance factor is fine.

00:01:14.411 --> 00:01:18.113
Delete 17: the search goes 10

00:01:18.113 --> 00:01:20.770
15, 20, 17.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.676
17 is a leaf

00:01:22.676 --> 00:01:24.620
so it is simply removed.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.798
20 drops to height 1

00:01:26.798 --> 00:01:28.664
with balance -1.

00:01:28.664 --> 00:01:30.687
15 keeps height 2

00:01:30.687 --> 00:01:32.709
and nothing else changes.

00:01:32.709 --> 00:01:33.720
No rotation.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.352
Back to the original tree

00:01:36.352 --> 00:01:38.561
and delete 15 instead.

00:01:38.561 --> 00:01:40.613
15 has two children.

00:01:40.613 --> 00:01:42.822
Its successor is 17

00:01:42.822 --> 00:01:45.900
the smallest key in its right subtree.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:48.518
Copy 17 into the node

00:01:48.596 --> 00:01:50.942
then delete the leaf 17

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.819
from the right subtree.

00:01:52.819 --> 00:01:55.165
20 has balance -1

00:01:55.165 --> 00:01:58.371
the new 17 has balance -1

00:01:58.371 --> 00:02:00.404
and nothing else changes.

00:02:00.404 --> 00:02:01.890
Again no rotation.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.209
Now delete 12 from this tree.

00:02:05.209 --> 00:02:07.865
17 loses its left child.

00:02:07.948 --> 00:02:10.936
It has an empty left subtree against

00:02:11.019 --> 00:02:14.090
the subtree at 20, of height 1

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.667
balance -2.

00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:19.070
So 17 is unbalanced on the right.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.407
Let y be its right child, 20.

00:02:22.407 --> 00:02:25.122
20 has balance -1

00:02:25.122 --> 00:02:29.014
it is heavy on the same side as 17.

00:02:29.014 --> 00:02:31.820
That is the right-right shape.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.303
A single left rotation at

00:02:34.388 --> 00:02:36.862
17 repairs it: 20

00:02:36.862 --> 00:02:39.932
with children 17 and 30.

00:02:39.932 --> 00:02:40.785
Above it

00:02:40.785 --> 00:02:44.283
10 has subtrees of heights 2 and 1

00:02:44.283 --> 00:02:45.818
balance +1.

00:02:45.818 --> 00:02:46.330
Fine.

00:02:47.003 --> 00:02:50.391
Now a bigger tree, of height 4.

00:02:50.391 --> 00:02:52.483
Delete 9, a leaf.

00:02:52.833 --> 00:02:55.918
Node 5 is left with the subtree at 2

00:02:55.918 --> 00:02:57.975
of height 1, on the left

00:02:57.975 --> 00:03:01.208
and nothing on the right: balance +2

00:03:01.208 --> 00:03:02.089
left-heavy.

00:03:02.089 --> 00:03:05.321
Its left child, 2, has balance -1

00:03:05.321 --> 00:03:07.599
it is heavy on the other side.

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:10.537
That is a dog-leg, the left-right case

00:03:10.537 --> 00:03:12.153
so a double rotation.

00:03:12.503 --> 00:03:15.640
A left rotation at 2 lifts 3.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.583
A right rotation at 5 lifts 3 again.

00:03:19.583 --> 00:03:21.914
The subtree is now 3

00:03:21.914 --> 00:03:24.423
with children 2 and 5.

00:03:25.003 --> 00:03:27.031
But we are not finished.

00:03:27.031 --> 00:03:30.074
The subtree at 5 had height 2

00:03:30.074 --> 00:03:33.877
and the new subtree at 3 has height 1.

00:03:33.961 --> 00:03:36.497
So the root 10 now has a left

00:03:36.581 --> 00:03:39.370
subtree of height 1 and a right

00:03:39.455 --> 00:03:43.089
subtree of height 3: balance -2.

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:45.878
The imbalance has propagated up.

00:03:45.878 --> 00:03:48.244
The tall side is the right

00:03:48.244 --> 00:03:49.850
so y is 17

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:52.554
and 17 has balance 0.

00:03:52.554 --> 00:03:55.597
Single rotation or double rotation?

00:03:55.597 --> 00:03:57.963
Pause the video and decide.

00:04:01.543 --> 00:04:04.044
A single left rotation at 10.

00:04:04.044 --> 00:04:06.377
17 becomes the root

00:04:06.377 --> 00:04:08.711
10 becomes its left child

00:04:08.711 --> 00:04:11.212
and 17's left subtree

00:04:11.212 --> 00:04:12.796
the one at 12

00:04:12.796 --> 00:04:16.463
moves across to become 10's right subtree.

00:04:16.813 --> 00:04:19.293
Two rotations for one deletion

00:04:19.293 --> 00:04:21.453
a double rotation at 5

00:04:21.453 --> 00:04:23.533
then a single one at 10.

00:04:23.613 --> 00:04:26.013
The final tree has height 4

00:04:26.093 --> 00:04:28.093
and passes the AVL check.

00:04:28.093 --> 00:04:29.533
Unlike insertion

00:04:29.533 --> 00:04:31.853
one rotation was not enough.

00:04:32.433 --> 00:04:36.081
In insertion we chose the rotation by comparing

00:04:36.159 --> 00:04:38.720
the new key with the child's key.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.281
In deletion there is no new key

00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:44.075
so the choice is made from heights.

00:04:44.153 --> 00:04:46.249
Let z have balance +2

00:04:46.326 --> 00:04:48.189
and y be its left child.

00:04:48.189 --> 00:04:50.673
If y's balance is 0 or more

00:04:50.673 --> 00:04:53.467
it is left-left: a single rotation.

00:04:53.467 --> 00:04:55.718
If y's balance is −1

00:04:55.718 --> 00:04:58.589
it is left-right: a double rotation.

00:04:58.589 --> 00:05:01.073
The mirror image for −2.

00:05:01.423 --> 00:05:03.920
Balance 0 at y never happens

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.530
after an insertion

00:05:05.611 --> 00:05:08.108
because the new key made one of

00:05:08.188 --> 00:05:10.524
y's subtrees strictly taller.

00:05:10.524 --> 00:05:13.503
But it does happen after a deletion

00:05:13.503 --> 00:05:14.389
as at 10.

00:05:14.389 --> 00:05:17.047
Then a single rotation is right

00:05:17.047 --> 00:05:20.590
it leaves z at +1 and y at −1.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:23.329
A double rotation can fail there

00:05:23.329 --> 00:05:25.503
leaving ±2.

00:05:25.853 --> 00:05:27.629
The slides show this same

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.405
tree fixed a second way

00:05:29.476 --> 00:05:31.253
by a double rotation that

00:05:31.324 --> 00:05:32.673
puts 12 on top.

00:05:32.673 --> 00:05:35.515
That happens to give a valid tree here

00:05:35.515 --> 00:05:36.794
but only by luck.

00:05:36.865 --> 00:05:38.996
Always choose from the child's

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:40.133
balance factor.

00:05:40.713 --> 00:05:42.783
Both insertion and deletion

00:05:42.859 --> 00:05:45.005
can use one method, balance

00:05:45.005 --> 00:05:47.918
which makes this choice from heights.

00:05:47.995 --> 00:05:50.218
If the left side is more than

00:05:50.294 --> 00:05:52.287
one taller than the right

00:05:52.287 --> 00:05:55.353
the allowed imbalance, t is left-heavy.

00:05:55.703 --> 00:05:57.812
Then compare the heights of

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.921
t.left's two subtrees.

00:05:59.921 --> 00:06:02.186
Greater or equal: left-left

00:06:02.186 --> 00:06:04.060
rotate with left child.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.403
The equal case, balance 0

00:06:06.403 --> 00:06:08.512
takes the single rotation.

00:06:08.512 --> 00:06:10.309
Otherwise: left-right

00:06:10.309 --> 00:06:12.183
double with left child.

00:06:12.533 --> 00:06:15.634
The right side is the mirror image

00:06:15.634 --> 00:06:19.177
and at the end t recomputes its height.

00:06:19.266 --> 00:06:22.632
For insertions this test is equivalent

00:06:22.721 --> 00:06:25.733
to the slides' comparison of keys.

00:06:26.313 --> 00:06:27.802
And here is remove.

00:06:27.802 --> 00:06:30.545
Search left or right, recursively.

00:06:30.624 --> 00:06:32.896
When the key is found and the

00:06:32.975 --> 00:06:34.699
node has two children

00:06:34.699 --> 00:06:37.363
copy the successor's key into it

00:06:37.442 --> 00:06:39.401
then remove the successor

00:06:39.479 --> 00:06:41.282
from the right subtree

00:06:41.282 --> 00:06:43.633
with a second recursive call.

00:06:43.983 --> 00:06:45.974
With zero or one child

00:06:45.974 --> 00:06:49.003
the node is replaced by its child

00:06:49.003 --> 00:06:50.042
or by null.

00:06:50.128 --> 00:06:52.206
And every call ends with

00:06:52.292 --> 00:06:54.023
return balance(t).

00:06:54.373 --> 00:06:56.340
So balance runs on every node

00:06:56.408 --> 00:06:59.054
of the search path, from the bottom up

00:06:59.054 --> 00:07:01.971
exactly walk back up, correct the heights

00:07:01.971 --> 00:07:03.666
and rotate where needed.

00:07:03.666 --> 00:07:05.701
When a successor was removed

00:07:05.769 --> 00:07:07.736
the nodes on that second path

00:07:07.804 --> 00:07:09.093
are rebalanced too.

00:07:09.673 --> 00:07:11.465
The cost of deletion.

00:07:11.465 --> 00:07:14.194
The search down is O(log n)

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.753
finding the successor at most

00:07:16.839 --> 00:07:18.545
another O(log n)

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:21.104
and each node on the way back

00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:23.066
up does constant work.

00:07:23.066 --> 00:07:25.710
So deletion costs O(log n).

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:27.587
But unlike insertion

00:07:27.587 --> 00:07:30.999
there may be a rotation at every level

00:07:30.999 --> 00:07:33.473
up to O(log n) rotations.

00:07:33.823 --> 00:07:36.582
A large random test checks the claims.

00:07:36.654 --> 00:07:39.703
After 100,000 random insertions

00:07:39.776 --> 00:07:41.445
the height is 19

00:07:41.445 --> 00:07:44.639
below the bound of 23.9.

00:07:44.712 --> 00:07:46.890
After 50,000 deletions

00:07:46.962 --> 00:07:49.866
it is 17, and the tree passes the

00:07:49.939 --> 00:07:51.463
AVL check every time.

00:07:51.813 --> 00:07:53.700
The worst case for deletion

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:55.167
is a Fibonacci tree

00:07:55.167 --> 00:07:58.241
where every node already leans to one side.

00:07:58.311 --> 00:08:01.526
Deleting the largest key of the Fibonacci tree

00:08:01.595 --> 00:08:04.111
of height 6 takes 3 rotations

00:08:04.111 --> 00:08:06.137
one per level on the way up.

00:08:06.137 --> 00:08:08.373
It is an exercise in the notes.

00:08:08.953 --> 00:08:11.848
So, the pros and cons of AVL trees.

00:08:11.848 --> 00:08:12.510
Search

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:16.150
insertion and deletion are all O(log n)

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:19.128
always, and the balancing adds only

00:08:19.211 --> 00:08:20.700
a constant factor.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.271
On the other hand

00:08:22.271 --> 00:08:25.828
AVL trees are harder to program and debug

00:08:25.828 --> 00:08:29.302
four rotation cases, and deletion on top.

00:08:29.385 --> 00:08:31.453
And each node needs extra

00:08:31.536 --> 00:08:33.273
space for its height.

00:08:33.623 --> 00:08:35.823
In practice, red-black trees

00:08:35.823 --> 00:08:38.098
which rotate less on updates

00:08:38.174 --> 00:08:40.752
are often preferred when there are

00:08:40.828 --> 00:08:43.104
many insertions and deletions.

00:08:43.104 --> 00:08:46.137
AVL trees shine when searches dominate.

00:08:46.137 --> 00:08:47.123
And on disk

00:08:47.123 --> 00:08:50.233
where every node visited is a disk read

00:08:50.233 --> 00:08:53.494
databases use B-trees: many keys per node

00:08:53.494 --> 00:08:55.694
so the tree is very shallow.

00:08:55.694 --> 00:08:56.983
Both come later.

00:08:57.563 --> 00:08:58.382
To sum up.

00:08:58.382 --> 00:09:01.656
One: delete as in a binary search tree

00:09:01.738 --> 00:09:04.767
then walk back up and rotate wherever

00:09:04.848 --> 00:09:07.550
the balance is +2 or −2.

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:10.660
Two: choose from the child's balance.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:14.098
Zero or the same side: a single rotation.

00:09:14.098 --> 00:09:16.963
The other side: a double rotation.

00:09:17.313 --> 00:09:20.076
Three: the imbalance can propagate

00:09:20.155 --> 00:09:22.759
so a deletion may need a rotation

00:09:22.838 --> 00:09:24.022
at every level.

00:09:24.022 --> 00:09:26.153
That is still O(log n).

00:09:26.503 --> 00:09:27.461
Try this.

00:09:27.461 --> 00:09:30.973
Build an AVL tree from 1, 2

00:09:30.973 --> 00:09:32.570
up to 15

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:35.976
a perfect tree with root 8.

00:09:35.976 --> 00:09:39.701
Then delete 1, 3, 2, 5

00:09:39.701 --> 00:09:42.894
7 and 6, in this order.

00:09:42.894 --> 00:09:46.406
Which deletions cause rotations

00:09:46.406 --> 00:09:50.983
and what is the child's balance each time?

00:09:51.333 --> 00:09:53.832
Next lecture: red-black trees

00:09:53.832 --> 00:09:57.413
the balanced trees inside Java's TreeMap.

